/node2/node3 holding application keys\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"No etcd package or binary pre-existed on any node (before_prepare snapshots and cmd-928e09ac84694e43/cmd-e0fa43ce2ea6415b/cmd-d4f58fa194de4e64 show only ssh listening). Executor installed etcd 3.5.17 from upstream binaries on all three nodes and bootstrapped a 3-member cluster; cmd-700a474bc12340ad shows member list with node1/node2/node3 all 'started'. Five /app/* keys were written and endpoint health for all three endpoints reported healthy (cmd-671f3fba966a4abb), and node3's own endpoint independently returned all five keys before any disruption (cmd-322424b258ad4b94), confirming a genuinely replicated healthy baseline.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-700a474bc12340ad\",\"cmd-671f3fba966a4abb\",\"cmd-322424b258ad4b94\",\"global:before_prepare:node1:listening-sockets\",\"global:before_prepare:node2:listening-sockets\",\"global:before_prepare:node3:listening-sockets\"]},{\"id\":\"req-2\",\"requirement\":\"Simulate/represent the node3 member as permanently lost with unrecoverable data\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"etcd was stopped and disabled on node3 (cmd-3d55123c718f4f4f), the member was removed from the cluster via the surviving quorum on node1/node2 (cmd-db861f2016a9439e, member list dropping to 2 started members), and node3's data directory was wiped with rm -rf, confirmed empty afterward (cmd-efc450dc989e4d96), making the prior member state irrecoverable exactly as the task describes.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-3d55123c718f4f4f\",\"cmd-db861f2016a9439e\",\"cmd-efc450dc989e4d96\"]},{\"id\":\"req-3\",\"requirement\":\"Cluster and data remain available on the surviving quorum during the node3 outage\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"With node3 stopped, removed, and wiped, the remaining node2 endpoint reported healthy and returned all five original /app/* keys (cmd-07bccd78f7f9478d), directly demonstrating that the 2-member quorum stayed available and consistent during the disruption window.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-07bccd78f7f9478d\"]},{\"id\":\"req-4\",\"requirement\":\"Replace the lost member with a working member on node3, restoring the cluster to three healthy members with existing keys intact\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"A new member was added for node3 via `member add` (cmd-d6235041d79448af), node3 was reconfigured with ETCD_INITIAL_CLUSTER_STATE=existing and its etcd service started successfully (cmd-1cb3b18190494263). Post-join verification shows member list with 3 started members, all endpoints healthy, and matching raft term/index across all three nodes (cmd-bb7d083a686844c1), and node3's own endpoint directly returned all five original keys, confirming full data resync (cmd-5739a817fa474172).\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-d6235041d79448af\",\"cmd-1cb3b18190494263\",\"cmd-bb7d083a686844c1\",\"cmd-5739a817fa474172\"]}],\"confidence\":0.9,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.82,\"summary\":\"Nearly all state-changing commands map directly to task-required setup, the required outage simulation, the replacement, or the mandatory restart protocol. All state-changing actions (package install, firewalld service additions, systemd unit/config creation, member remove/add, data wipe, and node reboots) are traceable to a required outcome or the fixed restart protocol, and no failed/conflicting services or unsafe exposure resulted (after_executor failed-services checks are empty on all three nodes). The one recurring minor issue is leftover build residue in /tmp on all three nodes — the downloaded tarball (/tmp/etcd.tar.gz), the extracted etcd-v3.5.17-linux-amd64 directory, and /tmp/etcd_start.log and /tmp/reboot.log were never cleaned up after installation completed (visible in the after_executor temporary-files diff for node1, node2, and node3). This is low-risk, bounded residue with no functional or security impact, but it was retained rather than reverted.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-1c87c0ede6a249a5\",\"cmd-fc95efd8cafd4cef\",\"global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files\",\"global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files\",\"global:after_executor:node3:temporary-files\",\"global:after_executor:node1:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node2:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node3:failed-services\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"The executor built the etcd cluster and its application keys from scratch (nothing pre-existed per the before_prepare snapshot), then faithfully enacted the task's described failure scenario on node3 (stop, remove from cluster, irrecoverable data wipe) while directly demonstrating that the surviving quorum stayed healthy and data-complete throughout. It then added a fresh node3 member with ETCD_INITIAL_CLUSTER_STATE=existing, and directly verified the restored cluster had three started members with matching raft term/index and all five original keys present on every endpoint including node3 itself. All four material outcomes are satisfied with direct, non-conflicting evidence. Independently of the public task, the mandatory node-restart evidence protocol was also completed for all three nodes with a coherent chain of pre-restart boot id + health, reboot acknowledgement, connection-drop confirmation, a distinct post-restart boot id, and full recovered cluster/data verification; these boot ids are corroborated by the fixed before_prepare/after_executor lifecycle snapshots for all three nodes, adding independent cross-check confidence.\",\"limitations\":[\"Leftover installation residue (tarball, extracted directory, start/reboot logs) was left in /tmp on all three nodes rather than cleaned up; low-risk but unaddressed.\",\"Node3's pre-restart boot-identity/health capture for the restart protocol (cmd-f9139291e6944a45) occurred after the member-replacement work rather than at the very start, which is appropriate for the restart protocol's own baseline but means it does not double as evidence of the original pre-disruption node3 boot state (that is separately covered by req-1 evidence).\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"req-1","requirement":"Establish a healthy three-member etcd cluster across node1/node2/node3 holding application keys","status":"satisfied","summary":"No etcd package or binary pre-existed on any node (before_prepare snapshots and cmd-928e09ac84694e43/cmd-e0fa43ce2ea6415b/cmd-d4f58fa194de4e64 show only ssh listening). Executor installed etcd 3.5.17 from upstream binaries on all three nodes and bootstrapped a 3-member cluster; cmd-700a474bc12340ad shows member list with node1/node2/node3 all 'started'. Five /app/* keys were written and endpoint health for all three endpoints reported healthy (cmd-671f3fba966a4abb), and node3's own endpoint independently returned all five keys before any disruption (cmd-322424b258ad4b94), confirming a genuinely replicated healthy baseline.","evidence_ids":["cmd-700a474bc12340ad","cmd-671f3fba966a4abb","cmd-322424b258ad4b94","global:before_prepare:node1:listening-sockets","global:before_prepare:node2:listening-sockets","global:before_prepare:node3:listening-sockets"]},{"id":"req-2","requirement":"Simulate/represent the node3 member as permanently lost with unrecoverable data","status":"satisfied","summary":"etcd was stopped and disabled on node3 (cmd-3d55123c718f4f4f), the member was removed from the cluster via the surviving quorum on node1/node2 (cmd-db861f2016a9439e, member list dropping to 2 started members), and node3's data directory was wiped with rm -rf, confirmed empty afterward (cmd-efc450dc989e4d96), making the prior member state irrecoverable exactly as the task describes.","evidence_ids":["cmd-3d55123c718f4f4f","cmd-db861f2016a9439e","cmd-efc450dc989e4d96"]},{"id":"req-3","requirement":"Cluster and data remain available on the surviving quorum during the node3 outage","status":"satisfied","summary":"With node3 stopped, removed, and wiped, the remaining node2 endpoint reported healthy and returned all five original /app/* keys (cmd-07bccd78f7f9478d), 